subsection.p:
Now cut the cylinder into small coordinate pieces. One piece has:
\Delta r \quad\text{in the radial direction,}
\Delta\theta \quad\text{in the angular direction,}
and
\Delta z \quad\text{in the vertical direction.}

image.latex-image:
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0, >=stealth]
    \draw[->] (-0.4,0) -- (5.2,0) node[right] {\(x\)};
    \draw[->] (0,-0.4) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {\(y\)};

    \draw[gray!25] (0,0) circle (1.8);
    \draw[gray!25] (0,0) circle (2.5);

    \draw[thick, fill=blue!10]
        (35:1.8) arc[start angle=35,end angle=60,radius=1.8]
        -- (60:2.5) arc[start angle=60,end angle=35,radius=2.5]
        -- cycle;

    \draw[->, thick] (0,0) -- (35:2.9) node[right] {\(r\)};
    \draw[->, thick] (1.0,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=35,radius=1.0];
    \node at (1.2,0.35) {\(\theta\)};

    \draw[<->] (60:1.8) -- (60:2.5);
    \node[above left] at (59.9:2.1) {\(\Delta r\)};

    \draw[<->] (35:2.75) arc[start angle=35,end angle=60,radius=2.75];
    \node[above] at (47:2.95) {\(r\,\Delta\theta\)};

    \node at (2.5,-0.85) {a small polar rectangle has area about \(r\,\Delta r\,\Delta\theta\)};
\end{tikzpicture}

subsection.p:
A cone whose height decreases linearly with distance from the axis also becomes simple. Consider a pile of sand whose top surface is z=4-\sqrt{x^2+y^2}, above the floor z=0. In cylindrical coordinates, this is z=4-r. The solid is 0\le\theta\le2\pi,\qquad 0\le r\le4,\qquad 0\le z\le4-r. Its volume is
V \amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^4\int_0^{4-r} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta \amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^4 (4-r)r\,dr\,d\theta \amp= 2\pi\int_0^4(4r-r^2)\,dr \amp= 2\pi\left[2r^2-\frac{r^3}{3}\right]_0^4 \amp= 2\pi\left(32-\frac{64}{3}\right) \amp= \frac{64\pi}{3}.
So \boxed{V=\frac{64\pi}{3}.}

statement.p:
The pipe is 1\le r\le2,\qquad 0\le\theta\le2\pi,\qquad 0\le z\le5. Its mass is
M \amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^5\int_1^2 (4+r)r\,dr\,dz\,d\theta \amp= 2\pi\cdot5\int_1^2(4r+r^2)\,dr \amp= 10\pi\left[2r^2+\frac{r^3}{3}\right]_1^2 \amp= 10\pi\left(8+\frac83-2-\frac13\right) \amp= 10\pi\cdot\frac{25}{3} \amp= \frac{250\pi}{3}.
So \boxed{M=\frac{250\pi}{3}\text{ kg}.}

subsection.p:
The bounds still describe the solid. The coordinates have changed, but the habit from
 stays the same:
\boxed{\text{Draw the shadow first.}}

image.asymptote:
import graph3;
size(11cm);
currentprojection=orthographic(-7,-10,8);

real radius=2, theta=pi/4, r=1.35;
triple P=(r*cos(theta),r*sin(theta),0);
triple Q=(r*cos(theta),r*sin(theta),3+r*cos(theta));
path3 base=(radius,0,0);
path3 roof=(radius,0,5);
for(int k=1; k<72; ++k) {
  real t=2pi*k/72;
  base=base--(radius*cos(t),radius*sin(t),0);
  roof=roof--(radius*cos(t),radius*sin(t),3+radius*cos(t));
}
base=base--cycle;
roof=roof--cycle;

draw(surface(base),rgb(0.38,0.70,0.43)+opacity(0.38));
surface wall=surface(new triple(pair q) {
  real t=q.x, u=q.y;
  return (2*cos(t),2*sin(t),u*(3+2*cos(t)));
},(0,0),(2pi,1),48,8);
draw(wall,rgb(0.36,0.60,0.84)+opacity(0.20));
surface top=surface(new triple(pair q) {
  real t=q.x, s=q.y;
  real x=2*s*cos(t), y=2*s*sin(t);
  return (x,y,3+x);
},(0,0),(2pi,1),48,10);
draw(top,rgb(0.35,0.60,0.88)+opacity(0.52));
draw(base,rgb(0.16,0.50,0.22)+linewidth(0.9pt));
draw(roof,rgb(0.13,0.35,0.63)+linewidth(1.0pt));

draw(O--(2.7,0,0),gray(0.42),Arrow3);
draw(O--(0,2.7,0),gray(0.42),Arrow3);
draw(O--(0,0,5.7),gray(0.42),Arrow3);
label("$x$",(2.7,0,0),2E);
label("$y$",(0,2.7,0),2N);
label("$z$",(0,0,5.7),2N);
draw(O--P,rgb(0.14,0.32,0.72)+linewidth(1.1pt),Arrow3);
label("$r$",0.55*P,2S,rgb(0.14,0.32,0.72));
path3 angle=(0.55,0,0);
for(int k=1; k<16; ++k) {
  real t=theta*k/15;
  angle=angle--(0.55*cos(t),0.55*sin(t),0);
}
draw(angle,gray(0.28)+linewidth(0.7pt));
label("$\theta$",(0.73*cos(theta/2),0.73*sin(theta/2),0),2N);
draw(P--Q,rgb(0.79,0.15,0.12)+linewidth(1.8pt),Arrow3);
dot(P,rgb(0.79,0.15,0.12));
dot(Q,rgb(0.79,0.15,0.12));
label("$D:0\le r\le2$",(-0.7,-1.05,0),2S,rgb(0.13,0.43,0.20));
label("$z=3+r\cos\theta$",Q,2E,rgb(0.13,0.35,0.63));

statement.p:
The solid is 0\le z\le3,\qquad 0\le\theta\le2\pi,\qquad 0\le r\le 1+\frac z3. Its volume is
V \amp= \int_0^3\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{1+z/3} r\,dr\,d\theta\,dz \amp= \int_0^3 \pi\left(1+\frac z3\right)^2 dz \amp= \pi\int_0^3 \left(1+\frac{2z}{3}+\frac{z^2}{9}\right)dz \amp= \pi\left[ z+\frac{z^2}{3}+\frac{z^3}{27} \right]_0^3 \amp= 7\pi.
Thus \boxed{V=7\pi.}
